WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.090
Let's go to our caller from the nine one three
area code.

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:07.009
Who's calling today from nine one three?

00:00:07.009 --> 00:00:08.009
Hi, David.

00:00:08.009 --> 00:00:09.790
Can you hear me?

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:11.179
Yes, I can.

00:00:11.179 --> 00:00:12.179
Who's this?

00:00:12.179 --> 00:00:13.179
Cool.

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:16.299
This is Andrew from the Boston area.

00:00:16.299 --> 00:00:22.710
My question for you is, what can be done to
sever the ties between property taxes and

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:24.050
school funding?

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:26.240
That's my final question for.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:27.240
Yeah.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:32.680
This is a great question, because you have
this perpetuating cycle in a lot of areas

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:39.820
where the richer areas with higher property
values are often collecting more property

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:40.940
taxes.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:46.440
And those taxes are used to fund schools which
make those schools better, which makes more

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:51.239
people want to move into those areas, which
often drives up property values even more,

00:00:51.239 --> 00:00:53.070
making the properties less accessible.

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:57.340
And you get situations that essentially mean
the interview earlier this week with Daniel

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:03.129
Markovits about the idea of a meritocracy,
not not being what we had, not also not being

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:08.110
ideal in its extreme form, but also not being
what we have is is related exactly to this

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:09.110
issue.

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:10.260
So I've seen different proposals.

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:14.720
I mean, in to put it very simply, you can
find other funding.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:21.070
You could you could just say property taxes
are no longer going to be directly funding

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:22.070
the schools.

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:27.100
So, for example, property taxes may be pooled
or partially pooled at the state level and

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:30.220
then redistributed out now not 100 percent.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:35.600
So if you have a place with really high property
taxes, they would send a portion of the taxes

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:36.640
to the state.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.780
A place with lower property tax receipts would
send a portion to the state, and then the

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:47.480
state would sort of redistribute more evenly
to try to improve the schools that that are

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:48.490
not quite so good.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:52.520
But, of course, the people that are paying
the higher property taxes are going to resist

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:53.520
that.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:57.200
They're going to say, wait, I'm paying more
so that my kids can go to good schools.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.680
And by living here, I'm actually helping somebody
somewhere else and that people are going to

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:02.950
be furious about that.

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:07.410
So if the question is how could it be done
mathematically, you'd redistribute.

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:09.500
You have a common pool and redistribute.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:10.899
If the question is how do you do it politically?

00:02:10.899 --> 00:02:14.030
I have no idea.

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:16.420
Yeah.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:21.920
Are there any leaders right now who are running
with this kind of at the top of their platform

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:22.920
that we can be promoting?

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.870
That's my second question.

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:27.239
And it's kind of hard not not that I'm aware
of.

00:02:27.239 --> 00:02:29.700
I mean, know it's it's certainly possible.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:33.170
I'm not aware of anybody who's who's making
that their issue, but that doesn't mean it

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:34.280
doesn't exist.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:36.530
All right, great.

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:38.030
Thanks, David.

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:40.739
All right, great to hear from you.

00:02:40.739 --> 00:02:41.739
Excellent question.

00:02:41.739 --> 00:02:43.620
Thank you for the call.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:48.129
Let's go to our caller from the nine eight
five area code.

00:02:48.129 --> 00:02:50.050
Who's calling today from 985?

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:56.359
Hi, my name is Christian Man, and I'm calling
from Baton Rouge, Louisiana.

00:02:56.359 --> 00:02:58.379
What's going on, Christian?

00:02:58.379 --> 00:03:00.400
Hey, how you doing?

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:05.310
It's kind of hard to hear you, but my question
was, so I listen to you and I also listen

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:06.859
to Ben Shapiro show.

00:03:06.859 --> 00:03:07.859
OK.

00:03:07.859 --> 00:03:13.550
And so it seems like you guys on some of the
same topics, like, for instance, like the

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:18.209
Trump speeches, you all have the exact opposite
points of view on this.

00:03:18.209 --> 00:03:19.209
Shocking.

00:03:19.209 --> 00:03:23.599
So I guess my question is, how is it how are
those opposite points of view from the two

00:03:23.599 --> 00:03:30.530
sides, like reconcilable in the current climate
where I don't think they are?

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:33.930
I mean, I think that it's sadly intractable.

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:37.459
I don't I don't watch Ben Shapiro, so I don't
know what he said.

00:03:37.459 --> 00:03:41.810
But earlier this week, I covered Tucker Carlson's
reaction to Trump's Mount Rushmore speech.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:47.019
And Tucker Carlson, I think, called it maybe
Trump's best speech ever.

00:03:47.019 --> 00:03:51.599
And what was so interesting to me about it
was it was the speech of rhetoric.

00:03:51.599 --> 00:03:53.440
It was not a speech of policy.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:58.739
So if the question if what you want to see
in a speech is what accomplishments does Trump

00:03:58.739 --> 00:04:02.300
claim from his first term and what is he going
to do in his second term?

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:04.390
You didn't get that in Trump's speech.

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:10.950
What you got from Trump's speech was dog whistle,
red meat rhetoric about we're fighting Marxists

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:14.909
and all these crazy, you know, straw men that
don't exist.

00:04:14.909 --> 00:04:21.720
The reason Tucker Carlson liked it was that
it was the type of rhetoric that Carlson himself

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:24.260
uses to bamboozle his audience.

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:26.220
It's essentially what Trump is doing.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:29.600
So we're looking at these things with completely
different standards.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:33.360
So I don't know if the views are reconcilable,
quite frankly.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:34.360
OK.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:39.100
All right, well, thank you, Ben, I appreciate
your time.

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:40.100
All right.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:41.100
No problem.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:44.630
It's a very pessimistic response, but I think
it's the you know, it's the reality.

